        "twitter_handle": null,
        "display_name": null,
        "comment": "Trimmed sample: these are the first 128,000 bytes (the complete PE image) of a 1,023,025,653-byte original, sha256 53c889d81914869958a2662518706e4f4dc47a22f380599c2967ea4b5d39288d. About 1 GB of random padding (overlay entropy ~7.98) was removed. The padding is there to get past AV and sandbox size limits.\n\nDelivery: fake TV torrent “VisionQuest S01E05 Firewall 1080p WEB H264-EZTV 7z” on LimeTorrents, 2026-10-21. The “.7z” is really a ZIP (sha256 ad4ab37a14f0e80e8d2d2109d3a980718ddb58583da530ec5311252a5c9514bb) containing vlc-3.0.23-win64_crypted.exe. A sibling lure, “VisionQuest S01E03 Reboot 1080p WEB H264-EZTV”, was seen on 2026-10-19.\n\nBehavior (Win11, isolated sandbox with fake internet): within ~40 s of launch it fetched two dead drops:\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/channel/UC18vFC1xgebddr_7NsvgBYA (@bubblegymes, “bubblegym”). The description holds “Config: [[OA0dDQ0HFBQHJyIHEkkQCAlJLS0JTgwAAB8S]]”, which is base64 + repeating XOR with key “ZhxchsdfhSGdfgsgdfAJsakdaks”, decoding to beenetprotect.com/lgz/gdata\nhttps://gist.githubusercontent.com/jasonterra69/48d9e264c05843f0cdc302143b4400c8/raw (now 404)\nThe dead-drop requests send YouTube consent cookies and a garbled binary User-Agent. It then POSTs “guid=” to https://beenetprotect.com/lgz/gdata, followed by a GET, with UA “Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 10.0; Win64; x64) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/152.0.0.0 Safari/537.36”.\n\nC2: beenetprotect.com, A record 109.120.179.122 (AS210644 AEZA GROUP, FR), registered 2026-09-14 via Cloudflare (DNS only, not proxied), Let’s Encrypt wildcard cert issued 2026-09-14. No next stage was observed, because the sandbox had no live C2."
      }
